\title{A Tropical Calculus for the Set-Theoretic Multiverse}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop an abstract, model-agnostic formalization of the set-theoretic
multiverse and establish a cross-domain bridge connecting quantification over
universes to tropical (min-plus) algebra. On the logical side, we axiomatize a
multiverse as a nonempty collection of universes together with a truth relation on
a type of statements, and we define the modes of multiverse truth: multiverse-true,
multiverse-false, possibly true, independent, and undetermined. Our first
structural result is that a statement is independent if and only if it is
undetermined --- it has no multiverse-wide truth value. We show that closure under
forcing alone forces a statement to be undetermined, giving a precise sense to the
slogan ``there is no true Continuum Hypothesis.'' On the algebraic side, we prove
that the map sending Boolean truth values into the tropical semiring
($\mathrm{true}\mapsto 1$, $\mathrm{false}\mapsto 0$) is a semiring homomorphism from
the Boolean semiring to the min-plus semiring, whence existential quantification over
a finite multiverse is a tropical sum and universal quantification is a tropical
product, and independence of a statement acquires the signature ``sum $=1$ and
product $\neq 1$.'' Finally we lift the bridge to a quantitative shortest-path
calculus in which each universe carries a real cost: the tropical sum computes the
cheapest witnessing universe (attained at an explicit cost-minimal witness) and the
tropical product computes the aggregate cost of a multiverse-truth, with the Boolean
theory recovered as the zero-cost slice. A concrete three-universe multiverse ($L$, a
Cohen extension, a measurable-cardinal universe) illustrates every result on the
statements ZFC, CH, $V=L$, and the existence of a large cardinal.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Cantor's Continuum Hypothesis (CH) asserts that there is no cardinality strictly
between that of the natural numbers and that of the real line. The combined work of
G\"odel (consistency of CH with ZFC, via the constructible universe $L$) and Cohen
(consistency of $\neg$CH, via forcing) established that CH is \emph{independent} of the
standard axioms: neither it nor its negation is provable. This is the paradigmatic
example of a mathematical statement without a truth value in the usual absolute
sense.

The \emph{set-theoretic multiverse} offers a stance toward such statements: rather
than a single privileged universe of sets, there is a plurality of universes, and
independent statements are simply true in some and false in others. A defining
structural feature is closure under forcing --- every universe has forcing
extensions, and forcing can flip the truth value of an independent statement.

This paper has two goals. The first is to make the multiverse picture precise enough
to prove its central slogans while remaining model-agnostic: we do not build models
of ZFC, but axiomatize exactly the data the multiverse view uses. The second, and the
technical novelty, is a bridge to tropical algebra. Existential and universal
quantification over a finite family of universes are, respectively, an iterated
logical OR and AND. Under a simple homomorphism into the min-plus semiring these
become a tropical sum and a tropical product, so that possibility and necessity are
read off two big operators, and independence has a clean tropical signature. We then
promote the bridge to a quantitative calculus: with real costs on universes, the same
operators solve shortest-path and total-cost optimization problems, recovering the
Boolean theory at zero cost.

\section{The abstract multiverse}

\begin{definition}[Multiverse]
A \emph{multiverse} $M$ consists of a type $\mathcal{U}$ of \emph{universes}; a type
$\mathcal{S}$ of \emph{statements}; a truth relation
$\holds : \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{S} \to \mathrm{Prop}$, where $u \holds s$ means
``$s$ is true in universe $u$''; together with a proof that $\mathcal{U}$ is
nonempty. No further structure is assumed: universes are opaque tokens and statements
need not be closed under connectives.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For a statement $s$ of a multiverse $M$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $s$ is \emph{multiverse-true}, $\MTrue(s)$, if $\forall u,\ u \holds s$;
  \item $s$ is \emph{multiverse-false}, $\MFalse(s)$, if $\forall u,\ u \not\holds s$;
  \item $s$ is \emph{possibly true}, $\Poss(s)$, if $\exists u,\ u \holds s$;
  \item $s$ is \emph{independent}, $\Indep(s)$, if
        $(\exists u,\ u \holds s) \wedge (\exists u,\ u \not\holds s)$;
  \item $s$ is \emph{undetermined}, $\Undet(s)$, if
        $\neg\MTrue(s) \wedge \neg\MFalse(s)$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
Let $s$ be a statement of a multiverse $M$.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item If $\MTrue(s)$ then $\Poss(s)$ (using nonemptiness).
  \item If $\Indep(s)$ then $\neg\MTrue(s)$ and $\neg\MFalse(s)$.
  \item No statement is both multiverse-true and multiverse-false.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
(1) Choose any universe $u$; if $s$ holds everywhere it holds at $u$. (2) An
independent statement has a failing witness (so it is not multiverse-true) and a
holding witness (so it is not multiverse-false). (3) If $s$ held everywhere and
nowhere, then at any $u$ we would have both $u \holds s$ and $u \not\holds s$, a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Independence equals undeterminedness]\label{thm:indep}
For every statement $s$, $\Indep(s) \iff \Undet(s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If $s$ holds at $u$ and fails at $v$, then $s$ is not multiverse-true
(it fails at $v$) and not multiverse-false (it holds at $u$). ($\Leftarrow$) From
$\neg\MFalse(s)$, i.e. $\neg\forall u,\ u\not\holds s$, classical logic yields a
universe where $s$ holds; from $\neg\MTrue(s)$ it yields a universe where $s$ fails.
Hence $\Indep(s)$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:indep} is the formal counterpart of the slogan: for a genuinely
independent statement, the question of its truth is meaningless without first
specifying a universe.

\begin{remark}
One may relativize truth to a sub-multiverse cut out by a predicate $P$ on universes:
$\mathrm{MTrueOn}(P,s) := \forall u,\ P(u) \to u\holds s$. This is monotone, and
$\MTrue(s)$ is exactly truth on the full sub-collection $P \equiv \top$.
\end{remark}

\section{A concrete multiverse}

\begin{definition}\label{def:concrete}
Let $\mathcal{U} = \{L,\ \mathrm{Cohen},\ \mathrm{Meas}\}$ and
$\mathcal{S} = \{\mathrm{ZFC},\ \mathrm{CH},\ V{=}L,\ \mathrm{LC}\}$, where
$\mathrm{LC}$ abbreviates ``there exists a large cardinal.'' Truth is given by the
table
\[
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
 & \mathrm{ZFC} & \mathrm{CH} & V{=}L & \mathrm{LC} \\ \hline
L & \mathrm{T} & \mathrm{T} & \mathrm{T} & \mathrm{F} \\
\mathrm{Cohen} & \mathrm{T} & \mathrm{F} & \mathrm{F} & \mathrm{F} \\
\mathrm{Meas} & \mathrm{T} & \mathrm{T} & \mathrm{F} & \mathrm{T}
\end{array}
\]
with $u \holds s$ defined to be ``the table entry is $\mathrm{T}$.''
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Results on the concrete multiverse]\label{thm:concrete}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{ZFC is multiverse-true.}
  \item \textbf{CH is independent}, hence undetermined: there is no true CH.
        Explicitly $\neg\MTrue(\mathrm{CH})$ and $\neg\MFalse(\mathrm{CH})$, while
        $\Poss(\mathrm{CH})$ holds (witnessed by $L$).
  \item \textbf{$V=L$ is independent} and \textbf{the existence of a large cardinal
        is independent.}
  \item \textbf{ZFC is determined} (not undetermined).
  \item \textbf{$V=L$ and large cardinals are incompatible in every single
        universe}: no $u$ satisfies both $V=L$ and $\mathrm{LC}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each item is a finite check against the table. (1) Every ZFC entry is $\mathrm{T}$.
(2) CH is $\mathrm{T}$ at $L$ and $\mathrm{F}$ at Cohen; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:indep}. (3) $V=L$ is $\mathrm{T}$ at $L$, $\mathrm{F}$ at Cohen;
$\mathrm{LC}$ is $\mathrm{T}$ at Meas, $\mathrm{F}$ at $L$. (4) ZFC is
multiverse-true, hence not undetermined. (5) $V=L$ and $\mathrm{LC}$ are never both
$\mathrm{T}$ in a row.
\end{proof}

\section{Forcing closure}

\begin{definition}[Forcing closure]
A statement $s$ is \emph{forcing-closed}, $\FC(s)$, if
\[
\forall u,\ \exists v,\ \big(v \holds s \iff \neg(u \holds s)\big).
\]
From every universe $u$ there is a universe $v$ in which $s$ has the opposite truth
value.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Forcing closure entails independence]\label{thm:fc-indep}
If $\FC(s)$ then $\Indep(s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take any $u_0$ and its flip $v$. If $u_0 \holds s$ then $s$ holds at $u_0$ and fails
at $v$; if $u_0 \not\holds s$ then $s$ holds at $v$ and fails at $u_0$. Either way $s$
has a holding and a failing witness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Forcing closure kills multiverse truth]\label{thm:fc-undet}
If $\FC(s)$ then $\Undet(s)$; in particular $\neg\MTrue(s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorems~\ref{thm:fc-indep} and~\ref{thm:indep}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:fc-undet} is the precise content of ``there is no true CH'':
closure under forcing alone precludes any multiverse-wide answer.

\begin{theorem}[Concrete forcing facts]
In the multiverse of Definition~\ref{def:concrete}, CH is forcing-closed while ZFC is
not.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From $L$ and Meas (CH true) the Cohen extension (CH false) is the flip; from Cohen
(CH false), $L$ (CH true) is the flip; hence $\FC(\mathrm{CH})$. Were ZFC
forcing-closed, from $L$ there would be a universe with ZFC false, contradicting that
ZFC is true in all three universes.
\end{proof}

\section{The tropical bridge}

\begin{definition}[Tropical semiring]
The \emph{tropical semiring} on $\Nbar = \mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$ (resp.\ $\Rbar$)
has carrier the tropicalized values $\trop(x)$, addition $\oplus = \min$, and
multiplication $\odot = +$. Its additive unit is $0_{\trop} = \trop(\infty)$ and its
multiplicative unit is $1_{\trop} = \trop(0)$. We write $\bigoplus$ and $\bigodot$ for
tropical sums and products.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Define $\beta : \mathrm{Bool} \to \trop(\Nbar)$ by
$\beta(\mathrm{true}) = 1_{\trop} = \trop(0)$ and
$\beta(\mathrm{false}) = 0_{\trop} = \trop(\infty)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[$\beta$ is a semiring homomorphism]\label{thm:hom}
For all $a,b \in \mathrm{Bool}$,
\[
\beta(a \vee b) = \beta(a) \oplus \beta(b) = \min(\beta a, \beta b),
\qquad
\beta(a \wedge b) = \beta(a) \odot \beta(b) = \beta a + \beta b.
\]
Moreover $1_{\trop} \le \beta(b)$ for every $b$, and $\beta(b) = 1_{\trop}$ iff
$b = \mathrm{true}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
All identities are verified by exhausting the four cases of $(a,b)$; the order and
unit facts by the two cases of $b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantifier--big-operator correspondence]\label{thm:qbo}
Let $\iota$ be a finite type and $p : \iota \to \mathrm{Prop}$ decidable. Then
\[
\Big(\bigodot_{i} \beta(\llbracket p\, i\rrbracket)\Big) = 1_{\trop}
\iff \forall i,\ p\,i,
\qquad
\Big(\bigoplus_{i} \beta(\llbracket p\, i\rrbracket)\Big) = 1_{\trop}
\iff \exists i,\ p\,i,
\]
where $\llbracket\cdot\rrbracket$ is the Boolean decision of a proposition.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For the product: each factor satisfies $1_{\trop} \le \beta(\cdot)$, so a tropical
product equals the unit iff every factor does, and $\beta(\cdot) = 1_{\trop}$ iff the
proposition holds. For the sum: untropicalizing sends $\bigoplus$ to $\inf$ over
$\Nbar$; the infimum of values ($0$ where $p$ holds, $\infty$ where it fails) equals
$0$ iff some value is $0$, i.e.\ iff some $p\,i$ holds.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Multiverse quantifiers are tropical operations]
For a finite multiverse $M$ and a statement $s$ with decidable per-universe truth,
\[
\MTrue(s) \iff \Big(\bigodot_{u} \beta(\llbracket u\holds s\rrbracket)\Big) = 1_{\trop},
\qquad
\Poss(s) \iff \Big(\bigoplus_{u} \beta(\llbracket u\holds s\rrbracket)\Big) = 1_{\trop}.
\]
Multiverse-truth is the tropical product of truth values; possibility is the tropical
sum.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Tropical signature of CH]
In the concrete multiverse,
\[
\bigoplus_{u} \beta(\llbracket u\holds \mathrm{CH}\rrbracket) = 1_{\trop}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\bigodot_{u} \beta(\llbracket u\holds \mathrm{CH}\rrbracket) \neq 1_{\trop},
\]
whereas ZFC has both its tropical sum and product equal to $1_{\trop}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
CH holds at $L$, so the tropical sum is the unit by Theorem~\ref{thm:qbo}; CH fails at
Cohen, so not all factors are the unit and the product is not the unit. ZFC holds at
all three universes, making both operators the unit.
\end{proof}

Thus independence has the tropical signature $\Sigma = 1 \wedge \Pi \neq 1$;
multiverse-truth is $\Sigma = 1 \wedge \Pi = 1$; multiverse-falsehood is
$\Sigma \neq 1$.

\section{The weighted (quantitative) calculus}

\begin{definition}[Weighted value]
Let $\iota$ be a type, $p : \iota \to \mathrm{Prop}$ decidable, and $c : \iota \to
\mathbb{R}$ a cost. The \emph{weighted cost} of $i$ is
$\wcost(i) = c(i)$ if $p\,i$ and $\infty$ otherwise, an element of $\Rbar$, and its
\emph{weighted value} is $\wval(i) = \trop(\wcost(i))$. For finite $\iota$,
\[
\cheapest(p,c) = \bigoplus_i \wval(i), \qquad
\necessityCost(p,c) = \bigodot_i \wval(i).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Untropicalizations]
$\untrop\,\cheapest(p,c) = \inf_{i} \wcost(i)$ and
$\untrop\,\necessityCost(p,c) = \sum_{i} \wcost(i)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Boolean bridge is the zero-cost slice]
With $c \equiv 0$, $\wval(i) = \beta(\llbracket p\,i\rrbracket)$ for every $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Possibility equals finite cheapest cost]\label{thm:poss-finite}
$\untrop\,\cheapest(p,c) = \infty \iff \forall i,\ \neg p\,i$; equivalently
$(\exists i,\ p\,i) \iff \untrop\,\cheapest(p,c) \neq \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The infimum of the $\wcost$ values is $\infty$ iff every value is $\infty$, i.e.\ iff
$p$ holds nowhere.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Attained cheapest witness]\label{thm:witness}
If $\exists i,\ p\,i$, then there is $i_0$ with $p\,i_0$,
$\untrop\,\cheapest(p,c) = c(i_0)$, and $\forall j,\ p\,j \Rightarrow c(i_0) \le c(j)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Restrict to the nonempty finite set $S$ of witnesses; an $\mathrm{argmin}$ of $c$ over
$S$ exists, call it $i_0$. Non-witnesses contribute $\infty$ and do not lower the
infimum, so $\inf_i \wcost(i) = \min_{j\in S} c(j) = c(i_0)$, minimal among witnesses.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity]
If $c(i) \le c'(i)$ for all $i$, then
$\untrop\,\cheapest(p,c) \le \untrop\,\cheapest(p,c')$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Necessity equals finite aggregate; total cost]
$(\forall i,\ p\,i) \iff \untrop\,\necessityCost(p,c) \neq \infty$, and when $p$ holds
everywhere, $\untrop\,\necessityCost(p,c) = \sum_i c(i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A finite sum in $\Rbar$ is $\infty$ iff some summand is $\infty$, i.e.\ iff $p$ fails
somewhere; when $p$ holds everywhere every $\wcost(i) = c(i)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Costed CH]
Take $\{L,\ \mathrm{Cohen},\ \mathrm{Meas}\}$ with CH true at $L$ and Meas and false
at Cohen, and costs $c(L)=0$, $c(\mathrm{Cohen})=1$, $c(\mathrm{Meas})=5$. Then CH is
possible, its cheapest witnessing cost is $0$ (attained at the zero-cost ground model
$L$), yet CH is not multiverse-true because it fails at Cohen.
\end{example}

\section{Applications and discussion}

$\MTrue$ and $\Poss$ are exactly the modal operators $\Box$ and $\Diamond$ for the
total accessibility relation on universes. Under $\beta$, $\Box$ and $\Diamond$ become
tropical matrix--vector products; over a general Kripke frame with edge costs,
iterated modalities become min-plus matrix powers and ``eventually accessible''
becomes the tropical matrix star (Kleene star) --- the same computation as all-pairs
shortest paths. The cheapest-witness operator is a shortest-path/Viterbi functional,
and monotonicity is the standard comparison principle for shortest paths. Independence
is upgraded from a bare impossibility to a quantity: the cheapest world realizing $s$
against the cheapest world realizing $\neg s$. The one structural subtlety is that
$\infty$-costs must be excluded from the argmin, which is why
Theorem~\ref{thm:witness} takes a possibility hypothesis; without it the infimum is
$\infty$ and no real witness exists --- precisely the criterion of
Theorem~\ref{thm:poss-finite}.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Conjecture 1 (Independence has a strictly positive optimality gap).} For a
statement independent across a costed multiverse, the cheapest cost of the statement
and of its negation are both finite, and their maximum is a genuine numerical
invariant --- the \emph{independence price} --- invariant under cost-preserving
isomorphisms. Independence is the coexistence of two finite min-plus witnesses, so it
acquires a scalar magnitude rather than a yes/no answer.

\textbf{Conjecture 2 (Modal accessibility is tropical matrix reachability).} With a
Kripke frame carrying edge costs, necessity/possibility over the accessible set are
entries of min-plus matrix powers of the weighted adjacency matrix, and the
reachability closure equals the tropical matrix star.

\textbf{Conjecture 3 (The cheapest-witness map is a valuation).} The assignment
sending a costed statement to its cheapest witnessing cost is a min-plus valuation:
the cost of a disjunction is the minimum of the costs, and the cost of a conjunction
is bounded below by their maximum, with equality characterized by a shared optimal
universe.

Further extensions include real-coefficient degrees of truth, a full Kripke-frame
modal bridge to min-plus matrix closure, genuine ZFC models replacing the abstract
truth relation by first-order satisfaction, Boolean-valued models generalizing $\beta$
to a homomorphism out of a complete Boolean algebra, and larger multiverses indexed by
finite families of forcing iterations.

\section{Conclusion}

We gave a model-agnostic account of the set-theoretic multiverse in which independence
is provably equivalent to the absence of a multiverse truth value, and closure under
forcing alone forces undeterminedness. We then bridged multiverse quantification to
tropical algebra: possibility is a tropical sum, necessity a tropical product, and
independence has the signature $\Sigma = 1 \wedge \Pi \neq 1$. Weighting the universes
turns these operators into a shortest-path calculus, with the Boolean theory recovered
at zero cost.

\end{document}

